1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,080
 In this demo, we'll be creating a program that utilizes the switch statement.

2
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:12,240
 Once you're ready, fire up Visual Studio Code, and let's go.

3
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:17,160
 Once we're in Visual Studio Code, click on the demo folder, or on the switch demo.

4
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:23,480
 So scroll down to the switch folder, and then double click on switch dot go.

5
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:29,240
 So you'll see we have a little bit of code here already.

6
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:33,240
 We have a price function, which returns one, and some constants.

7
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:36,000
 We have an economy, business, and first class.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,400
 We haven't covered cons very much, but it's just like a variable, except you can't change it.

9
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:42,840
 So const is just short for constant.

10
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:48,720
 In our main function, we're going to create a switch statement.

11
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:54,360
 So we'll switch on p, and we'll create that variable, and we'll create the value from the price function.

12
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:57,880
 We'll then create some cases.

13
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:00,200
 So we'll use case expressions.

14
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:05,680
 So in the case is less than two, when p is less than two, we'll print out a message.

15
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:10,280
 So we'll have a cheap item for the message.

16
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:15,680
 If the case is less than 10, we'll say that it's moderately priced.

17
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:21,160
 And the default case will be expensive item.

18
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:29,400
 So remember with cases, they're checked from top to bottom.

19
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:34,680
 So if it's less than two, and this executes, then this one here will not execute.

20
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:39,360
 So you won't get cheap item followed by moderately priced item.

21
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:40,400
 That won't happen.

22
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:44,640
 So if we have the case of p, and the price is one, for example.

23
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:48,960
 So if we scroll up, we'll see that our price function does return one.

24
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:55,840
 So this case will be executed first, and then nothing else gets checked after that.

25
00:01:56,680 --> 00:01:59,840
 We're going to create another case.

26
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:04,920
 So we'll create a ticket, and this will be an economy seating ticket.

27
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:07,040
 We'll then switch on the ticket.

28
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:13,760
 And the case of an economy ticket will print out economy seating.

29
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:20,880
 In the case of a business ticket, we'll print out business seating.

30
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:27,720
 And in the case of first class, we'll print out first class seating.

31
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:32,640
 Any other tickets will have a default.

32
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:35,280
 I'll just print out other.

33
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:43,120
 We're using the word economy here, because that's defined as a constant here.

34
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:44,920
 So it's like I said, it's just like a variable.

35
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:49,840
 So we have economy, business, and first class, zero, one, and two.

36
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:51,160
 So we're just working with integers.

37
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:56,840
 So when we switch on the ticket, if the ticket is economy, we'll get economy seating.

38
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:01,640
 It's business, business seating, first class, first class seating, anything else,

39
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:02,880
 then we're going to get other seating.

40
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:07,600
 If we take a look at our code, we'll see that the first thing that prints out

41
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:12,160
 should be cheap item, because our price is actually equal to one when we call the

42
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:12,600
 function.

43
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:17,560
 And we have an economy ticket, and so we should get economy seating printed out.

44
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:20,400
 So it should be a cheap item followed by economy seating.

45
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:23,760
 Now I'm going to hop into a terminal, and we'll give it a go.

46
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:29,400
 You can run this with gorun.slash demo slash switch.

47
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:31,880
 And there we go.

48
00:03:31,880 --> 00:03:35,000
 We have a cheap item and economy seating.

49
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,880
 This concludes the demo on the switch statement.

50
